                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Cute characters, great art, horrible puzzles.\n\nThe \"Super Mario Galaxy\" of traditional Sierra / LucasArts style adventure games has your characters walking around circular planetoids. Unfortunately, the puzzles they encounter are so illogical that a guide is absolutely required to make progress. Some locations even exist purely to confuse the player, and serve no true purpose. It's a real shame.\n\nI judge games by their gameplay, and Tales of Cosmos fails miserably, falling into all the bad habits of the old adventure genre, with bizarre solutions that no human being could ever guess. Even if you intend to randomly try things for hundreds of hours, sometimes you have to try the \"thing\" on the \"thing\" multiple times before you get the desired outcome with no feedback. \"Try every object on every object, then try it again\" is the marching order, and even then you're unlikely to ever make some of the logical leaps. Out of frustration, you will launch your slow spacecraft for another planet to see if you missed something, and watch your characters shuffle in slow motion, like geriatrics, around this beautiful scenery. For some solutions, you have to do the \"thing\" at the right time. How likely are you to make that leap? To stand in the same room, doing nothing, waiting for an event to happen? Not likely.\n\nThe character movement is extremely slow, and flying between planets takes far too long as well. It feels like planets were placed a fair distance away from each other to artificially extend gameplay. Even the old Sierra adventures had game-speed controls.\n\nI would like to recommend it, because it is extremely cute and contains great art and animations (I love it when the little puppy fogs up his space helmet from panting). These characters deserve a better game. If you want to play through a short, cute adventure using a walkthrough (there is a steam community guide, thank god), go for it, but if you like to solve puzzles on your own, this romp through the cosmos will infuriate you.",
